    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d919">

                <pb facs="0856" n="848" xml:id="pg848"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/623: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-04-08T18:00:00+08:00">April 8, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 8—6:52 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>50. My 43, March 26, 4 p.m.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d919-f1"/> Barrier conditions
                    have not improved; congestion and delays are probably greater particularly for
                    British; some sentries stop cars at a distance and insist that drivers,
                    including Americans, alight to present passes and wait for permission to
                    proceed.</p>
                <p>Almost daily oral representations are made to Japanese consular and military
                    authorities, and on March 27th I wrote to the Japanese Consul General making
                    specific reference to the delays being encountered by American owned cargo
                    covered by consular certificates and requesting that, in conformity with the
                    repeated assuraces of the Japanese authorities, such goods be passed promptly.
                    The letter has not been answered and no beneficial effect can be observed.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping. Air mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
